<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Taranchi Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1864 CE – 1871 CE · capital Ghulja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Muslim sultanate in the Ili valley that rose during the Dungan revolt and was occupied by Russia in 1871.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1864 a rebellion of Hui (Dungan) and Turkic Muslims against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> rule spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. In the Ili valley, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranchi">Taranchis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> farmers descended from settlers brought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungan-people">Dungans</a>, captured the Qing garrisons of Ili, including Huiyuan, and established a sultanate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Ghulja</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taranchi">Taranchi</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abil-oghlu">Abil Oghlu</a> became sultan in 1866 after the Taranchis turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungan-people">Dungans</a>. The sultanate ruled a farming population in the Ili valley, under Islamic law, and dealt with Russian merchants and neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yettishar">Yettishar</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Fearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-beg">Yaqub Beg</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yettishar">Yettishar</a> would take Ili and alarmed by instability on its frontier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> occupied the Ili valley in 1871, ending the sultanate. Russia returned most of the region to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> of 1881.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taranchi-sultanate.jpg" alt="Taranchi Sultanate" width="320" height="145"><figcaption>Image: Qingkuan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_at_the_Wei_River_(Weihe).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1864 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ghulja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abil Oghlu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uyghur</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ili Sultanate, Kulja Sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=taranchi-sultanate" aria-label="Taranchi Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6557 764 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taranchi Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6877 1020L7567 1068M6877 1020L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6877 1020h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6877 1020h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=taranchi-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
